\title{The Converse Cost Curve: A Unified Cost--Information Plane for Factor-Revealing Witnesses of Semiprimes}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{19 September 2026}

\begin{abstract}
Let $N=pq$ be a semiprime with distinct primes $p\neq q$. A \emph{factor-revealing witness} is a quantity attached to $N$ whose value, together with $N$, determines the unordered pair $\{p,q\}$; a \emph{definition route} is the cost of evaluating that witness by its defining computation. We study four classical witnesses --- the gcd-sum $M_1(N)=\sum_{x<N}\gcd(x,N)$, the first hit of the zero-divisor scan, congruences of squares, and the count of idempotents modulo $N$ --- and prove that they occupy a single cost--information plane. We establish: the exact closed form $M_1(pq)+2(p+q)=4pq+1$ and, more generally, the four-cell formula $S_f(pq)=f(pq)+(q-1)f(p)+(p-1)f(q)+(p-1)(q-1)f(1)$ valid for every local gcd-statistic $S_f(N)=\sum_{x<N}f(\gcd(x,N))$, together with the affine equivalence $M_1(N)+1=2\varphi(N)+2N$; the exact cost $\min(p,q)$ of the zero-divisor scan, its exact hit count $p+q-1$, its exact miss count $\varphi(N)$, and the same $\Omega(\min(p,q))$ bound for uniform random probing; two counting ladders, namely exactly $2^{\omega(N)}$ idempotents modulo any $N>0$ and exactly $2^{\omega(N)}$ square roots of unity modulo any odd $N>0$, whence both counters are constant on semiprimes and carry zero factorisation information while any individual nontrivial witness yields a factor by one gcd; a \emph{no-polylogarithmic-route} theorem, stating that for every degree $d$ there is a threshold beyond which every route cost of every balanced semiprime exceeds $(\log_2N)^d$; and a black-box converse in the adaptive gcd-probe model, showing that for any strategy, budget and output rule two semiprimes with disjoint factorisations yield identical transcripts, so no bounded-query gcd-probe algorithm factors. The results form a converse for a family, not an unconditional hardness theorem.
\end{abstract}

